8cu -- upstream 9front's 8c, linked against APExp's shared cc/.

WHAT THIS IS FOR
----------------

`objtype=386 mk install' stops on

	ar.c:586 bad in naddr: REGPAIR

and `sys/lib/tests/regpair-min.c' reduces that to two structs and one
assignment:

	void
	case1(Dst *d, Src *s)
	{
		d->offset = s->size;		/* both vlong */
	}

APExp's 8c fails on it.  Current 9front's 8c compiles it, and so does
every 64-bit backend.  Four hypotheses have been killed by measurement
-- register pressure, the two struct types differing, the member name,
and position in the file -- and reading has cleared all five of
APExp's own differences from upstream's 8c.  That leaves the shared
front end, `sys/src/cmd/cc/', and this directory is the one build that
says so or does not.

	8cu  =  upstream 8c sources  +  APExp cc.a

	it FAILS  -> the fault is in APExp's cc/, which is where every
	             other line of evidence already points.
	it PASSES -> the fault is in APExp's 8c/ after all, and the
	             reading that cleared those five changes is wrong
	             somewhere.  That would be worth more than a
	             confirmation.

HOW TO BUILD AND RUN IT
-----------------------

	cd sys/src/cmd/8cu
	mk install
	cd ../../../lib/tests
	8cu regpair-min.c

`mk install' builds `../cc' first -- `mkfile.port's `$LIB: $CCSRC' rule
does `cd ../cc; mk install' -- so the cc.a it links is APExp's current
one, not a stale copy.  That is the point of the experiment and not an
accident of the mkfile.

**Read the LINE NUMBER in any diagnostic.**  `regpair-min.c''s
assignment is on line 101.  A diagnostic naming any other line means
the file under test is not the one you think it is; that has already
happened once in this investigation and cost a round.

WHERE THE SOURCES CAME FROM
---------------------------

github.com/9front/9front, sys/src/cmd/8c, at the commit whose log reads

	38c61773c  news(1): remove notes about trash handling

Copied verbatim.  The only file not taken from upstream is this mkfile,
which differs from upstream's in three ways and no more:

  - TARG=8cu rather than 8c, so it lands beside the real compiler in
    $objtype/bin instead of over it.
  - APEXPROOT and `<$APEXPROOT/sys/src/cmd/mkone' rather than
    `</sys/src/cmd/mkone', which is this tree's idiom everywhere.
  - OFILES drops `8c_vla.$O'.  That file is APExp's, it does not exist
    upstream, and nothing in cc/ calls the one function it defines
    (`gvlalloc' is declared in cc.h and called only from APExp's own
    `OALLOCA' case).  `gvla_prologue' and `gvla_epilogue' have weak
    defaults in `cc/vla.c'.

WHAT IT DOES *NOT* NEED, which was got wrong first
--------------------------------------------------

An earlier version of this plan said upstream's `ginit' must gain

	typecmplx = typesuv;

or `cc/pgen.c' would dereference a nil pointer on the first function
with a return value.  **That is wrong.**  `cc/sub.c's `tinit()' sets
`typecmplx = typesuv' itself, among the 32-bit defaults, and every
backend's copy of the line is redundant.  No shim of any kind is
needed, and following that instruction would have meant editing a
vendored file for no reason and then wondering whether the edit was
load-bearing.

NOT IN THE DEFAULT BUILD
------------------------

`sys/src/cmd/mkfile's DIRS does not list this directory, on purpose.
One failing entry aborts the whole tree's mk, and these sources have
never been compiled on this machine -- APExp's `cc.h' is a superset of
the one upstream's 8c was written against, which ought to be enough and
has not been shown to be.  **The first build is the VM's**, the same
weaker position every `cc/' change ships from.

If it does not compile, that is itself informative and belongs in
`docs/notes/kencc.md' beside the rest: it would mean upstream's backend
has moved past APExp's front end in some way the diff did not show.

WHEN IT HAS SERVED ITS PURPOSE
------------------------------

Delete it.  A second compiler in the tree is a maintenance cost and a
thing to be confused by, and this one answers exactly one question.
